<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carpathian Ruthenia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Subcarpathian Ruthenia, Transcarpathia, Zakarpattia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carpathian Ruthenia is a region on the southern slopes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathian Mountains</a>, part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> for centuries. Assigned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> as Subcarpathian Ruthenia, it remained its poorest province. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> annexed it in 1939, and in 1945 it was ceded to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>; it is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>'s Zakarpattia Oblast.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carpathian-ruthenia.jpg" alt="Carpathian Ruthenia" width="320" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zakarpattia_in_Ukraine.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Saint-Germain added <b>Subcarpathian Ruthenia</b>, and the Treaty of Trianon in 1920 set the border with Hungary.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Munich Agreement of 1938 weakened Czechoslovakia, <b>Subcarpathian Ruthenia</b> received autonomy in October 1938, first under Andriy Brodiy and then the Greek Catholic priest Avgustyn Voloshyn.</blockquote><a href="/state/carpatho-ukraine">Carpatho-Ukraine</a></li><li><blockquote>In November 1918, as Hungarian rule over <b>Transcarpathia</b> collapsed, a council of Hutsuls in the mountain village of Yasinia took power locally.</blockquote><a href="/state/hutsul-republic">Hutsul Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>As the Austro-Hungarian Empire collapsed, Ukrainian soldiers seized Lviv on 1 November 1918, and a Ukrainian National Council proclaimed the West Ukrainian People's Republic over eastern Galicia, northern Bukovina and <b>Transcarpathia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/west-ukrainian-peoples-republic">West Ukrainian People's Republic</a></li></ul></section>
